{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And he said, Whose daughter are you.</b> In its explanation he already said, for certainly he did not give her the nose-ring and the bracelets until he had asked her. And so you will find in the servant's account that the question precedes the gifts — that is what he said: ״ואשאל אותה ואומר בת מי את״ and afterward ״ואשים הנזם על אפה״. And like it: ״ויברכם וירד״, whose meaning is 'and they had already descended from making the sin-offering and the burnt-offering.'" "<b>To lodge.</b> It is a noun and its meaning is one night's lodging. And so the Rabbis of blessed memory explained: Rivkah changed the expression and said ללון, which is an infinitive form serving for all times alike, and her intention was apparently toward many lodgings. But the correct interpretation is that ללין is a verbal form whose pattern is להלין (to cause to lodge). And Scripture says: ״היש בית אביך מקום שתלינו אותנו״ — 'is there in your father's house a place that you would lodge us,' and we would take shelter in your shade, according to the custom of a guest with the master of the house. And Rivkah replied: ״גם מקום ללון״ — 'also a place to lodge' — you will not need us to lodge you; rather you yourself will choose a lodging place with us in whatever place you wish, according to the custom of the master of the house, not according to the custom of the guest. And from this she changed the expression and said ללון, which is a standing (i.e., infinitive) form."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:46.999691272Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}